AR(1)                          P32W Unix                           AR(1)

NAME
       ar - object archives

SYNOPSIS
       ar lib.a file.o ...
       ar [-]key[v] lib.a [file ...]

DESCRIPTION
       ar bundles objects made by as(1) or cc(1) into an archive (a
       library) for ld(1).

       In the first form ar writes a new lib.a holding the objects
       named; an existing lib.a is replaced, not added to. Each member
       is named after its file, without the directory; if two files
       have the same name, the later one wins. Members are stored in
       name order, after an index of the global symbols each
       defines. See ar(5) for the format. The first argument is taken
       as an archive name unless it is a key as below.

       In the second form a key letter, with or without a -, says
       what to do. Files are matched with members by their names
       without the directory.

       r      replace the members named by the files, or add them;
              lib.a is made if it does not exist.
       q      quick append: the same as r, since members are unique
              and kept in name order.
       t      list the members (all, or those named), one per line,
              each followed by the global symbols it defines, in name
              order:

                     a.o: alpha beta
                     b.o: gamma

       x      extract the members (all, or those named) into files of
              their names in the current directory.
       d      delete the members named.

       v      (with a key) verbose: r and q print a - name for each
              member added and r - name for each replaced, x prints
              x - name, d prints d - name, and t prints each member's
              size in bytes before its name.

       c and u are accepted after the key and ignored: a new archive
       is made without comment, and r always replaces.

EXAMPLES
       cc -c a.c b.c c.c
       ar libx.a a.o b.o c.o        make a library
       ar t libx.a                  see what is in it
       cc -c b.c; ar rv libx.a b.o  replace one member
       ar x libx.a c.o              get c.o back
       ar d libx.a a.o              delete a member
       cc -o prog prog.c libx.a     link with it

EXIT STATUS
       0 on success; 1 if a file cannot be read, is not an object,
       the archive is not an archive or cannot be written, or a file
       named for t, x or d is not a member; 2 for a usage error.

SEE ALSO
       ld(1), as(1), cc(1), ar(5), a.out(5)

BUGS
       There is no p (print) or m (move) key, and no a, b or i
       positioning: members are always in name order. The archive
       format is P32W's own, not the V7 or System V one.

       An archive whose name is made only of key letters (such as
       t or rv) cannot be made with the first form.
